Yes. A substantial part of law is exactly the disciplined study of standards, classifications, and interpretation—but law also adds authority, procedure, evidence, and consequences.

A useful compression is:

\text{Law} =
\text{Rules}
+
\text{Standards}
+
\text{Interpretation}
+
\text{Facts}
+
\text{Procedure}
+
\text{Authority}
+
\text{Remedy}

A rule tries to draw a relatively crisp boundary: “must be 18,” “file within 30 days.”

A standard deliberately leaves judgment in the loop: “reasonable care,” “good faith,” “material,” “probable cause,” “beyond a reasonable doubt,” “best interests.”

Then interpretation asks things like:

\text{What does this text mean?}

\text{Which standard governs this situation?}

\text{Do these facts satisfy that standard?}

That last move is especially central:

\text{facts}
\xrightarrow{\text{legal characterization}}
\text{standard}
\xrightarrow{\text{application}}
\text{conclusion}

Law school trains this constantly. You take a messy reality and ask which legally relevant distinctions survive.

That is why lawyers argue over words that seem absurdly ordinary: reasonable, substantial, knowingly, foreseeable, imminent, material, undue, necessary. Those words are interfaces between reality and a decision system.

And there are standards governing the interpretation itself: standards of review, burdens of proof, canons of statutory interpretation, precedent, jurisdiction, evidentiary standards, and so forth.

So in Pantheon language, law is interesting because it is a mature human system for doing something you’ve been circling:

Reality is continuous and messy; decisions require discrete cuts.

Law constructs those cuts, specifies who has authority to make them, gives interpreters methods for applying them, and provides mechanisms for challenging bad interpretations.

Which maps almost perfectly onto your Treaty Semantics principle:

classification authority ≠ ontological sovereignty.

A court saying something counts as “reasonable” or “property” or “speech” for purposes of a legal regime does not mean reality itself is obligated to use that ontology.

The classification has a jurisdiction.

That may be one of the deepest things Pantheon can mine from law: not merely rules, but an entire civilization-scale technology for governing interpretation under disagreement.